\hd{Bottom line}
\textbf{The network reaches 100k t only at its design point, and even there our P50 is <<plan_p50>> t (P10–P90 <<plan_p10>>–<<plan_p90>>; P(≥100k) <<plan_prob>>), because almost every nominal input sits near the favourable end of its range.} Q1 has to buy about <<contingency>> t of contingency. The cheapest tonnes are the ones we already produce and then lose at dMRV, at <<lev1_cost>>/t to recover against about <<lev2_cost>>/t for new capacity.

\hd{What has to be true}
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Throughput of <<plan_throughput>> t dry feedstock per kiln-day:} <<plan_dry>> t batches (a <<plan_kiln_m3>>\,m³-class kiln) at <<plan_bpd>> batches/day, the single-shift cycle ceiling. On published Kon-Tiki values throughput is <<lit_throughput>> t/kiln-day and the network delivers <<lit_net>> t. This is the first number to measure.
\item \textbf{Feedstock dried to ≤<<plan_moisture>> moisture.} The CH₄ deduction then falls from <<ch4_share_30>> to <<ch4_share_5>> of gross removals.
\item \textbf{About <<plan_pass>> of batches pass dMRV.} At the design point <<plan_loss_share>> of gross removals is lost before credit, mostly to rejections.
\item \textbf{Jan–Jun carries <<plan_h1_share>> of the year and the monsoon only <<plan_monsoon_share>>.} Slippage before June cannot be recovered. In our synthetic year, actuals ran at <<act_ratio>> of plan from January.
\end{itemize}

